\DOC LOCAL_MONTMUL_P384_TAC

\TYPE {LOCAL_MONTMUL_P384_TAC : int -> string list -> tactic}

\SYNOPSIS
Advances an {eventually}-style machine-state goal across one inlined
Montgomery-multiplication subroutine call, replacing the current symbolic state
with a later one and recording the arithmetic specification of the result as a
new assumption.

\DESCRIBE
The first argument, an {int}, selects which pointer-register setup variant
precedes the call; it controls how many register-assignment assumptions (such
as the one for the second argument pointer) are refreshed or added. The second
argument is a {string list} of three names identifying the destination buffer
and the two input operands of the multiplication. The conclusion retains its
shape {eventually ... s}, with the state variable {s} replaced by a
later-numbered state. Assumptions about the old state — program counter, loaded
code, register contents, memory contents at the various buffers, and the
accumulated {MAYCHANGE} relation — are removed and re-added for the new state,
with the {MAYCHANGE} term extended by the frame component for the destination
buffer and the program-counter assumption replaced by one at a larger offset.
Two assumptions are added: one giving the destination value in the form
{t <= ... ==> u = ...}, a conditional Montgomery-product equation for the named
result, and one memory-read assumption equating the destination buffer in the
new state with that result. Earlier purely arithmetic assumptions are
preserved, sometimes reordered.
{
   A, pc-and-state assumptions for s  ?- eventually ... s
   =================================================  LOCAL_MONTMUL_P384_TAC n ["dest"; "op1"; "op2"]
   A, pc-and-state assumptions for s', (t <= ... ==> u = ...), read-of-dest-in-s' = u  ?- eventually ... s'
}

\COMMENTS
The observed traces show only this single kind of transformation, on goals of
this one shape.

\ENDDOC
